IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `src-employee`

-- Safety, Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1988 -- worked cases, from the seat of the injured employee.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (C2026C00285), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act;
-- every case is HYPOTHETICAL.
--
-- Tom (HYPOTHETICAL), an APS officer working 38 hours a week, slips on a wet floor in his department's office and injures
-- his back. He is off work, then returns on reduced hours.

§ `cases`

§§ `ss 5, 5A, 5B, 6`

-- The arguments: employer; what was suffered; significant contribution (disease); where; otherwise arose; abnormal risk;
-- reasonable administrative action.
-- Tom's back injury at his workplace: an injury.
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `at my workplace for work, or on an ordinary break from it` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
-- Injured in a car accident driving from home to his usual office: s 6(1C) takes the journey out, and nothing else makes
-- it arise out of or in the course of his employment.
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `travelling between home and my usual workplace` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: it did not arise out of, or in the course of, my employment (ss 5A(1)(b), (c), 6)`
-- The same journey, but he was carrying classified files home on his manager's instruction (it otherwise arose in the
-- course of his work): an injury.
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `travelling between home and my usual workplace` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
-- Driving to a meeting in another city at his department's direction: an injury (s 6(1)(d)).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `a Commonwealth authority` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `travelling for work at my employer's direction or request` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
-- Hurt doing backflips off the office desk at lunch: an abnormal risk voluntarily and unreasonably taken (s 6(3)).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `at my workplace for work, or on an ordinary break from it` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: it did not arise out of, or in the course of, my employment (ss 5A(1)(b), (c), 6)`
-- Assaulted by a member of the public because of his job: an injury (s 6(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `by an act of violence that would not have happened but for my job` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
-- An anxiety disorder from sustained workload, to which his employment contributed to a significant degree: an injury.
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation, including a psychological condition that developed over time` TRUE `somewhere else` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`
-- The same, but his employment's contribution was only material, not substantially more: no (s 5B(3)).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation, including a psychological condition that developed over time` FALSE `somewhere else` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: a disease must have been contributed to, to a significant degree (substantially more than material), by my employment (s 5B)`
-- Distress from a reasonable performance appraisal reasonably carried out: excluded (s 5A(2)(a)).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `the Commonwealth, such as a department or agency` `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation, including a psychological condition that developed over time` TRUE `somewhere else` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no: it resulted from reasonable administrative action taken in a reasonable manner (s 5A(1), (2))`
-- A private employer's worker: the State or Territory scheme (not covered).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `another employer` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `at my workplace for work, or on an ordinary break from it` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `an employer that is not the Commonwealth, a Commonwealth authority or a licensed corporation is under a State or Territory workers compensation law, which is not encoded (s 5)`
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Act` `a licensed corporation (a self-insurer licensed under the Comcare scheme)` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `at my workplace for work, or on an ordinary break from it` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is an "injury" under s 5A`

§§ `s 14`

#ASSERT `Is Comcare liable` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: Comcare is liable to compensate me for the injury (s 14(1)), and for reasonable medical treatment (s 16)`
-- A sprain needing physiotherapy only, no time off: medical expenses only.
#ASSERT `Is Comcare liable` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `only medical expenses: the injury has not resulted in death, incapacity for work or impairment, but reasonable medical treatment is still compensated (ss 14(1), 16(2))`
#ASSERT `Is Comcare liable` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an intentionally self-inflicted injury is not compensated (s 14(2))`
#ASSERT `Is Comcare liable` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: an injury caused by my serious and wilful misconduct is not compensated unless it results in death or serious and permanent impairment (s 14(3))`
#ASSERT `Is Comcare liable` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: Comcare is liable to compensate me for the injury (s 14(1)), and for reasonable medical treatment (s 16)`

§§ `ss 53, 54`

#ASSERT `Have I made a claim that counts` TRUE FALSE TRUE `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation` TRUE
-- No medical certificate yet: not taken to be made (s 54(3)); but a claim for medical expenses needs none.
#ASSERT NOT `Have I made a claim that counts` TRUE FALSE TRUE `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation` FALSE
#ASSERT `Have I made a claim that counts` TRUE FALSE TRUE `medical expenses only (s 16)` FALSE
-- Notice given late because he did not know he had to: excused (s 53(3)); not excused: no claim.
#ASSERT `Have I made a claim that counts` FALSE TRUE TRUE `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Have I made a claim that counts` FALSE FALSE TRUE `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation` TRUE

§§ `s 16`

-- A 60 km return trip to a spinal specialist: claimable. A 40 km trip by car: not. A 10 km ambulance trip his injury
-- required: claimable. A 10 km bus trip it did not require: not.
#ASSERT `Can I claim travel to treatment` TRUE 60 FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Can I claim travel to treatment` TRUE 40 FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Can I claim travel to treatment` TRUE 50 FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Can I claim travel to treatment` TRUE 10 TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Can I claim travel to treatment` TRUE 10 TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Can I claim travel to treatment` FALSE 60 FALSE FALSE

§§ `ss 19, 23`

-- Tom's normal hours are 38: the maximum rate lasts while the hours lost total no more than 45 x 38 = 1,710.
-- The arguments: incapacitated; normal weekly hours; hours lost to date; hours employed this week; pension age reached;
-- injured within 2 years of pension age; weeks already compensated; imprisoned.
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 380 0 FALSE FALSE 10 FALSE EQUALS `a maximum rate compensation week: compensation is worked out on all of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(2))`
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 1710 0 FALSE FALSE 45 FALSE EQUALS `a maximum rate compensation week: compensation is worked out on all of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(2))`
-- Week 46, not working at all: 75%.
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 1748 0 FALSE FALSE 45 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 75
-- Back on 9.5 hours (exactly 25%): 80%. On 9.6 hours (just over 25%): 85%. On 19 hours (50%): 85%. On 28.5 hours (75%):
-- 90%. On 30 hours: 95%. On his full 38 hours but not at his old level: 100%.
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 2000 9.5 FALSE FALSE 60 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 80
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 2000 9.6 FALSE FALSE 60 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 85
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 2000 19 FALSE FALSE 60 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 85
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 2000 28.5 FALSE FALSE 60 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 90
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 2000 30 FALSE FALSE 60 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 95
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 2000 38 FALSE FALSE 60 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 100
-- Not incapacitated; in prison; at pension age (injured earlier); injured at 66 with pension age 67, after 104 weeks.
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` FALSE 38 380 0 FALSE FALSE 10 FALSE EQUALS `none: I am not incapacitated for work this week`
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 380 0 FALSE FALSE 10 TRUE EQUALS `none: I am imprisoned for an offence (s 23(2))`
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 3000 0 TRUE FALSE 80 FALSE EQUALS `none: I have reached pension age (s 23(1))`
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 3952 0 TRUE TRUE 104 FALSE EQUALS `none: the 104 weeks for an injury suffered within 2 years before pension age are used up (s 23(1A))`
-- Injured at 66, now past 67, after 60 weeks: still compensated, at the reduced rate (s 23(1A)).
#ASSERT `Which weekly rate applies` TRUE 38 2280 0 TRUE TRUE 60 FALSE EQUALS `a reduced rate week: compensation is worked out on this percentage of my normal weekly earnings less what I earn or could earn (s 19(3))` 75

§§ `ss 24 to 26`

-- A 12% whole person impairment of his back on the printed $80,000 maximum: $9,600. At 9%: nothing (s 24(7)).
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `another impairment` 12 0 80000 EQUALS 9600
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `another impairment` 9 0 80000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `another impairment` 10 0 80000 EQUALS 8000
-- On an indexed maximum of $85,000 (an assumed figure): 20% is $17,000.
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `another impairment` 20 0 85000 EQUALS 17000
-- The loss of a finger at 5%: no 10% threshold (s 24(8)), $4,000.
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `the loss, or loss of use, of a finger or toe, or the loss of the sense of taste or smell` 5 0 80000 EQUALS 4000
-- A hearing loss: 4% impairment, 6% binaural loss, $3,200; with a 4% binaural loss, nothing (s 24(7A)).
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `a hearing loss` 4 6 80000 EQUALS 3200
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `a hearing loss` 4 4 80000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation` `another impairment` 100 0 80000 EQUALS 80000

#ASSERT `Can I get more for a worse impairment` FALSE 10
#ASSERT NOT `Can I get more for a worse impairment` FALSE 9
#ASSERT `Can I get more for a worse impairment` TRUE 5
#ASSERT NOT `Can I get more for a worse impairment` TRUE 4

-- Assessed on 1 March 2026: payable by 31 March 2026, with interest after that (s 26).
#ASSERT `By when must my impairment compensation be paid` (Date 1 3 2026) EQUALS Date 31 3 2026

§§ `s 17`

-- On an assumed indexed lump sum of $600,000 (the Act prints $400,000).
#ASSERT `What lump sum is payable on death` TRUE TRUE 600000 EQUALS `the full lump sum, for all the dependants, shared as Comcare determines (s 17(3), (8))` 600000
#ASSERT `What lump sum is payable on death` FALSE TRUE 600000 EQUALS `an amount Comcare determines, up to this, for the partly dependent dependants (s 17(4))` 600000
#ASSERT `What lump sum is payable on death` FALSE FALSE 600000 EQUALS `no lump sum: the employee left no dependants (s 17(2)); medical and funeral costs are still payable (ss 16, 18)`

§§ `ss 62, 64`

-- The notice of determination reached Tom on 10 February 2026: ask for reconsideration by 12 March 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I ask for reconsideration` (Date 10 2 2026) EQUALS Date 12 3 2026
#ASSERT `Can I go to the Tribunal` FALSE EQUALS `first ask the determining authority to reconsider it, within 30 days (s 62); only the decision on reconsideration can go to the Tribunal (ss 60(1), 64)`
#ASSERT `Can I go to the Tribunal` TRUE EQUALS `I may apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal for review of the reconsideration decision (s 64(1)(a))`
